WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Sandbox policy should be tested like security-critical code.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:20.040
Tests should cover allowed operations, denied permissions, malformed IPC messages, unsupported commands, oversized input, timeout behavior, crash and restart paths, shutdown handling, and regression cases for policy changes.

3
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.340
The goal is to prove not only that useful work succeeds, but that denied work fails safely.

4
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.260
Monitoring makes containment visible in production.

5
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:42.840
Useful signals include sandbox launch failures, denied operations, unexpected permission requests, resource-limit events, worker crashes, timeout categories, restart counts, and IPC validation failures.

6
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.860
Logs should contain safe categories and component labels, not secrets or raw sensitive data.

7
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.720
Dashboards and alerts should help teams separate expected denials from regressions that block real work.

8
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.560
Updates can change sandbox assumptions.

9
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:06.380
Platform behavior, dependencies, helper binaries, policy files, installer behavior, and feature flags can all alter what a component can access.

10
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:13.280
New features should trigger permission review, and policy changes should be versioned, documented, and tied to release ownership.

11
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.960
Governance keeps the design from drifting.

12
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:23.820
Someone should own the sandbox policy, the broker contract, worker permissions, exception approvals, and monitoring expectations.

13
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:32.800
Reviewers should be able to answer what changed, why it changed, which tests cover it, and how operations will know if the new policy causes failures.

14
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.640
Treat sandbox configuration as part of the product's security architecture.

15
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.500
It is not a one-time setting.

16
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:48.640
It is a living boundary that needs tests, monitoring, documentation, and review as the application, platform, and dependency set evolve.

17
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:58.340
Good governance also defines who can approve exceptions, how long exceptions last, and which release notes or operational alerts need to mention policy changes.

18
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.880
The strongest programs make those questions routine before a release, not urgent after a failure.
